The convergence question and this companion
The question is what convergence ordinary full BFGS can guarantee when a convex objective has
gradient jumps. Its practical success invites a theory, but the smooth argument cannot simply
treat every secant as a bounded Hessian acting on a short step. Lewis–Overton’s experiments and
Challenge 7.1 place this problem in a broader almost-sure semialgebraic setting [2]. The present
work gives deterministic positive and negative answers for specified convex classes. It does not
claim to settle every part of that challenge.
The mixed objective F (u, v) = u2 + |v| gives a concrete measure of progress. Guo–Lewis developed
extensions of Powell’s smooth-convex argument and proved convergence on the Euclidean norm [10,
Theorems 3.2, 4.1, 4.3 and Corollary 4.2]. Their Corollary 4.4 proves only that a subsequence for
the mixed objective approaches a point on v = 0, leaving whole-sequence convergence to zero open.
The horizontal error u2 can remain positive on that line. Gebken’s published 2026 introduction still
identifies this benchmark as unresolved, while its criticality theorem assumes iterate and matrix
behavior [5, Theorem 3.1 and (B1)].
The centered smooth–sublinear result here proves that every infinite admissible full-BFGS run on
u2 + |v| converges to zero, for arbitrary positive-definite initialization and fixed valid Armijo–weak-
Wolfe parameters. It is a consequence of a family argument in arbitrary dimension. The sublinear
part is modeled exactly toward its center; adding a locally C 1,1 convex term leaves only a quadratic
inward remainder. Compact initial sublevels inside the structural region give value convergence,
and uniqueness gives convergence of the whole sequence. The same route covers coercive degree-
one homogeneous functions, including coercive finite maxima of linear functions. The Euclidean
norm is an attributed known case. The full noncompact homogeneous value alternatives are
retained separately: value convergence does not imply bounded iterates, and it does not subsume
Xie–Wächter’s finite detection of unbounded descent in the two-piece affine model [1, Theorem IV.1].
Local convergence extends this positive picture. Finitely many polyhedral cells with smooth
extensions supply a uniform inward model after a low sublevel traps the iterates near the minimizer.
The algorithm need not identify a cell. Curved switching sets can defeat that radial condition; the
planar theorem constructs a comparison field along positive contours instead. Its nondegenerate
smooth-max corollary and explicit three-quadratic example show a convergence conclusion beyond
the radial class. The fixed-normal theorem in arbitrary dimension and the flat-contour parabolic
family use different hypotheses and remain independent parts of the theory.
The negative results establish a complementary limitation. A fixed unit-first implementation can
generate a shrinking cycle around a nonstationary corner. The construction certifies every rejected
and accepted query, and then embeds copies so that one fixed objective has failing starts arbitrarily
close to its unique minimizer, all with identity initialization. One embedding has four globally
convex C 2,1 branches with bounded Hessians and no uniform positive lower-curvature assumption.
A different embedding preserves uniform strong convexity with C 1,1 branches that lose second
differentiability at the accumulation point. These are two distinct exclusions of deterministic
neighborhood guarantees. They neither establish positive-probability failure nor produce a finite
uniformly strongly convex C 2 local counterexample.
The common analytical tool is an adjacent-degree hierarchy for actual principal determinants of
full-BFGS matrices. Building on the classical trace/determinant approach [16, 15, 14], it forces
the arithmetic mean of reciprocal metric-gradient values to diverge under the stated bounded-
gradient and vanishing-decrease hypotheses. A comparison energy turns this matrix restriction
into convergence when objective geometry preserves visibility of nonoptimality, controls the secant
correction and permits transport at a summable cost. Matrix degeneration is allowed throughout.
The radial and moving-field arguments demonstrate ways this mechanism succeeds; the scalar

