1.1 Algorithm, quantifiers, and notions of convergence
The algorithm is ordinary full BFGS in finite-dimensional Euclidean space:

                gk = ∇F (xk ),       sk = −αk Hk gk ,          yk = gk+1 − gk ,             H0 ≻ 0,

                                                    !                    !
                                           sk y T               yk sT            sk sTk
                            Hk+1 =       I− T k         Hk    I− T k         +          .
                                           yk sk                yk s k           ykT sk

An infinite admissible run has nonzero gradients at differentiable accepted iterates, positive lengths,
and fixed Armijo–weak-Wolfe constants 0 < c1 < c2 < 1:

                          F (xk+1 ) ≤ F (xk ) + c1 gkT sk ,       T
                                                                 gk+1 sk ≥ c2 gkT sk .

No damping, restart, skipped update, memory truncation, proximal operation, imposed spectral
limit, or assumed iterate convergence is introduced. Positive theorems quantify over all such infinite
runs unless a more specific implementation is stated.
Three conclusions must remain distinct:

                          F (xk ) → F∗ ;       dist(xk , X∗ ) → 0;           xk → x∗ .


