On a compact sublevel, objective convergence implies distance to the solution set tends to zero.
Uniqueness then gives whole-sequence convergence. Affine-fiber nonuniqueness has a separate lifting
theorem; arbitrary nonunique solution sets do not inherit that conclusion.
A zero gradient at a differentiable point is successful termination. A ray with no differentiable
acceptable point is not an infinite admissible run. The positive statements must not silently be
strengthened into universal all-ray existence or finite oracle complexity.

1.2 The negative results that must both be retained
1.2.1 N1. Deterministic local failure with globally C2,1 convex branches
There is one fixed coercive F : R2 → R, with a unique nonsmooth minimizer at zero, of the form

                       F = max fi ,        fi ∈ C 2,1 (R2 ),    0 ⪯ ∇2 fi ⪯ 64I.
                            0≤i≤3

Every branch Hessian is globally Lipschitz. With the fixed initialization H0 = I, a sequence
of bad starts approaches the minimizer. The actual deterministic unit-first weak-Wolfe
doubling/bisection search produces nonstationary converged limits:

                (j)           (j)                       (j)
               x0 → 0,       xk → pj ̸= 0,         F (xk ) ↓ F (pj ) > 0,   0∈
                                                                             / ∂F (pj ).

The same objective, starts, and trial decisions work for every fixed 0 < c1 ≤ 1/3, c1 < c2 < 1. Every
accepted and rejected query is differentiable. Accepted lengths repeat 1, 1/4, 1/16, with at most five
trials per iteration. The entire query trajectory can remain in an arbitrarily small neighborhood of
the true minimizer. Initial objective gaps also tend to zero.
This is a failure of a deterministic neighborhood guarantee, not a claim that every close start
fails. It is proved by an exactly isolated algebraic cycle, exact matrix closure, a scalar convex
interpolation, all-trial containment, and scaling induction. Proof: Chapter 12.

1.2.2 N2. Deterministic local failure with positive lower curvature
There is also a strongly convex finite objective, with a unique nonsmooth minimizer and compact
sublevels, such that

                                       0 < µI ⪯ ∇2 F (x) ⪯ LI

wherever its classical Hessian exists, yet the same specified deterministic implementation admits
bad starts arbitrarily close to the minimizer. A single fixed affine change of coordinates makes the
common initial matrix I.
One version is a maximum of four globally C 1,1 , uniformly strongly convex, gradient-Lipschitz
branches. The individual branches are not twice differentiable at the accumulation point.
Another version has one constant SPD classical Hessian wherever it exists, but a countably
piecewise-affine nonsmooth component rather than finitely many smooth branches.
The source uses c1 = 10−4 , c2 = 1/2. Its bad seed is an existence object on a certified hyperbolic
stable manifold, not a displayed rounded point claimed to lie exactly on that manifold. Proof:
Chapter 13.
N1 and N2 are not merged into a fictitious stronger theorem. N1 has more branch
differentiability and no uniform positive lower curvature. N2 has positive lower curvature and less
differentiability at the accumulation point. Neither proves local failure for a finite maximum of
uniformly strongly convex C 2 branches through the optimizer.




