1.3 General positive mechanisms: retain distinct hypotheses
1.3.1 P1. Objective-level transported certificates
For differentiable endpoints define g = ∇F (x), y = ∇F (z)−g, s = z−x, d = −g T s, ∆ = F (x)−F (z),
and t = y T s. An eligible pair satisfies

                             d > 0,        c1 d ≤ ∆ ≤ d,      t ≥ (1 − c2 )d.

On each compact positive objective band, it suffices that a finite-valued PSD field W satisfy

                              g T W (x)g ≥ m > 0,          y T W (z)y ≤ Ct,


                                       Πs W (z)Πs ⪯ Πs W (x)Πs ,

for every eligible pair with sufficiently small d. The latter two constants and the field may depend
on the band and on the fixed line-search parameters. No continuity, uniform boundedness, or
positive definiteness of W is required.
Then every admissible run on the compact sublevel has value and solution-set convergence; unique-
ness gives iterate convergence. A local structural neighborhood gives a trapped low-sublevel basin.
A condition on one-sided shells around possible positive limiting levels suffices as well.
The normalization with exact projected nonexpansion is equivalent to allowing (1 + Ld): multiply
W by exp((L/c1 )(F − F∗ )). Proof: Chapter 3; normalization and geometry: Chapter 5.
RS is the radial specialization W (x) = (x − x∗ )(x − x∗ )T . Its equivalent inward Taylor condition is
useful for checking functions, but is not a separate strongest certificate theorem. The inclusion of
RS is strict. The transported condition itself is not necessary for actual convergence.

1.3.2 P2. Moving frames with controlled cumulative transport
A frame satisfying Vk+1 = Vk Tk + sk bTk has an exact BFGS comparison-energy identity. Uniform
visibility, bounded secant correction, and bounded cumulative transport products and inverses
exclude positive-level stagnation. This trajectory mechanism need not produce a single position-only
certificate. Proof: Chapter 2.

