1.4 Strongest concrete local and global positive families
1.4.1 P4. Radially regular and finite-partition geometry
On a compact positive band, RS is equivalent to uniformly quadratic inward first-order modeling
error:

                    0 ≤ F (x − θr) − F (x) + θ∇F (x)T r ≤ (Mη /2)θ2 ,        r = x − x∗ .

The constants may deteriorate as the band approaches the minimum. This yields local convergence
for a finite convex function whose neighborhood of a unique minimizer has a finite polyhedral
partition with C 1,1 extensions on each cell. Individual cell extensions need not be convex
outside their cells; the full objective is convex.
Consequences include finite convex PLQ objectives on polyhedral cells; smooth convex plus finite
polyhedral terms; appropriate absolute-value, hinge, quantile, fused and generalized ℓ1 terms; and
local mixtures of Euclidean group residuals. A finite maximum of arbitrary quadratics is not
automatically PLQ.
Centered local representations

                                        F (x∗ + z) = ψ(z) + γ(z)
                                  1,1
are covered when ψ is convex Cloc     and γ is finite convex degree-one homogeneous. A global
representation on a compact initial sublevel gives global value convergence; uniqueness gives iterate
convergence. Neither original summand must separately be minimized at the center.
Also retained are finite sums of powered nonnegative gauges, and increasing convex smooth
transforms of coercive gauges. Different powers, including 1 < p < 2, and coupled linear maps
are permitted under the stated source hypotheses. General spectral regularization at a nonzero
rank-deficient solution is not automatic. Proofs: Modules R and F.
For smooth-plus-polyhedral objectives, retain the explicit sufficient basin threshold obtained by
minimizing the objective over the active regions of pieces inactive at the optimizer. For nonunique
affine fibers, retain the exact linear-image lifting formula. These are not replaced by a generic
uniqueness statement.

1.4.2 P5. Planar affine-critical cluster points
Let F : R2 → R be finite convex, have an attained minimum and a compact sublevel, and have
strictly convex positive sublevels. Away from affine-critical points, assume either local convex C 2
smoothness or a local maximum of two convex C 2 branches whose active gradients are linearly
independent.
Then every infinite admissible run has an affine-critical cluster point. Adding no false affine
criticality on the local basin gives convergence. For finite smooth maxima, affine independence
