1.4.3 An explicit supersession established in this consolidation
The older theorem for

                          F (u, v) = max{a(u) + pv, b(u) − qv},       p, q > 0,

with convex C 2 profiles, coercive ϕ = (qa + pb)/(p + q) having unique minimizer u∗ , and a′′ , b′′ > 0
off u∗ , is a corollary of P5, not an independent strongest theorem.
Here is the implication. The profiles are strictly convex. If two different points of a positive sublevel
have different horizontal coordinates, strict profile convexity puts the interior of their segment
strictly below the level. If their horizontal coordinates agree, their vertical coordinates differ; the
two nonzero normal slopes again make every strict convex combination satisfy both inequalities
strictly. Thus every positive sublevel is strictly convex. At a tie, the determinant of the active
gradients is −qa′ (u) − pb′ (u) = −(p + q)ϕ′ (u). It is nonzero except at u∗ , where the tied point is
precisely the optimizer. Smooth points are already regular. Hence P5 applies globally, and its
localization argument gives the old local result.
This is a disclosed deduction between the stated theorems. The operative implication and its formal
proof are included here and in the Lean index; no unavailable independent archive is needed.

