For

                    F (u, v) = max{ai u2 + bi v},    ai > 0,    min bi < 0 < max bi ,
                               i≤m                                i               i

all admissible full-BFGS runs converge globally to zero. Branches with bi = 0 are allowed. Such
branches can create flat contour segments, so P5 does not subsume the whole family. Its explicit
certificate also proves strict inclusion beyond eligible-pair RS. Full proof: Chapter 10.

1.4.5 P7. Arbitrary-dimensional fixed-normal geometry
Let P = {x : aT x = b} be an exceptional hyperplane through a unique minimizer, with S = a⊥ .
On a neighborhood of a compact initial sublevel, assume strong convexity, a single-valued Lipschitz
tangential subgradient projection PS ∂F (x) = {A(x)}, and local C 1,1 regularity off P . Every
admissible run converges.
The full normal gradient need not have a uniform Lipschitz constant near the hyperplane. A local
version uses a low trapped sublevel. For F (u, v) = ψ(u, v) + h(v), full strong convexity can be
weakened to tangential strong convexity of ψ, strict positive contours, compactness, and a unique
optimizer with v∗ = 0.
A separate cluster theorem permits a closed collection of parallel genuinely nonsmooth inter-
faces. It forces one affine-critical cluster point under strong convexity and a Lipschitz tangential
projection. Add no false affine criticality to conclude optimality. The single-hyperplane theorem is
not a literal specialization: it allows differentiable but non-C 1,1 exceptional points.
The arbitrary-dimensional result is not a planar reduction or a block-diagonal algorithm. Its real
restriction is the fixed normal direction. The explicit normal-curvature-spike examples lie outside
RS. Complete proofs and infinite-series oracle: Chapter 11.



