1.4.6 P8. Scalar convergence
In one dimension, finite convex objectives with a unique minimizer and compact initial sublevel
have convergence of every infinite admissible positive-metric Armijo–weak-Wolfe run, independently
of the BFGS update. This remains nonredundant because it covers functions lacking every local
transported certificate. Proof and nonnecessity example: Chapter 5.

1.5 Noncompact homogeneous theory remains independent
For every finite convex degree-one homogeneous objective, every infinite admissible run satisfies

                                     F (xk ) → 0   or F (xk ) → −∞.

No iterate, step, matrix, or length bound is assumed, and the supporting gradient set may be
infinite. Bounded-below objectives therefore have optimal value convergence; coercive ones have
xk → 0.
For finite max-linear objectives, retain three refinements: a finite-value-limit run has zero in the
affine hull of its recurrent gradients; recurrent gradient span of dimension at most two gives
whole-iterate convergence on the finite-value branch; and purely flat-direction noncompactness
admits an explicit metric-projection limit. General affine-fiber lifting gives the same type of result
for reduced nonhomogeneous objectives.
The noncompact zero-value iterate branch in recurrent dimension at least three is not completely
classified. A compact-level certificate theorem cannot replace these noncompact statements. Full
proofs: Chapter 7.
The two-piece finite-escape theorem from Xie–Wächter is pre-existing background and remains
stronger in its finite-termination conclusion than the new many-piece infinite-run escape statement.
The user’s paper is supplied separately, not rebranded as a new result.

