1.7 Geometric findings retained independently of convergence
The condition

                                    0 ∈ aff ∂F (p) =⇒ 0 ∈ ∂F (p)

means that every nonoptimal nearby point admits a direction along which all subgradients predict
the same strictly negative rate. Affine independence and strictly positive optimal coefficients make
this true locally for finite smooth maxima, with uniform positive-band affine separation. Pointwise
separation alone need not be uniform when ranks change.
For finite convex semialgebraic objectives, the affine-critical set has finitely many connected
components, and the objective is constant on each. Thus only finitely many affine-critical values
occur, giving no false affine criticality below a sufficiently low threshold. This is a geometric
proposition, not a blanket BFGS theorem. Proofs: Chapter 8.
The incoming-jump obstruction rules out all PSD certificates when the target gradient is a vanishing-
cost linear combination of several eligible jumps arriving at the same target. It neither assumes
regularity of the certificate nor constructs one common BFGS history. Scalar accumulating-kink
examples show certificate failure despite convergence. These distinctions are retained in Chapter 5.

1.8 Verification and unresolved boundaries
The delivered verification records contain actual pinned Lean builds, exact theorem contracts,
module-wide declaration inventories and transitive axiom checks. Chapter 14 identifies the process
logs and reproduction commands. Earlier claims about unprovided scripts, old execution counts,
precisions and timings remain unsupported historical text in the original snapshot.
The exact deterministic cycle, its smooth embedding, the actual return-map derivative and stable
manifold, principal determinants, frame identities, geometric joining and infinite tail arguments
have Lean proofs. Their logical roles are distinct. A rounded initial point is not asserted to belong
to the stable manifold, and a finite numerical experiment is not used as a proof of an infinite
trajectory.
The remaining central gaps are: arbitrary-dimensional rotating-normal smooth-max geometry;
the general local theorem for finite uniformly strongly convex C 2 branches without the recorded
nondegeneracy/geometric assumptions; generic or positive-measure failure; other fixed line-search
implementations; arbitrary nonunique solution-set iterate convergence; noncompact high-rank
homogeneous zero-value iterates; and sharp rates or universal matrix asymptotics.
The blanket question is not answered by calling all semialgebraic functions safe: global failure
already occurs for affine maxima. The planar and fixed-normal positive theorems have precise
additional assumptions. The latest lower-curvature counterexamples likewise do not establish local
failure in a finite analytic or semialgebraic description.




