Relation to earlier work
The following comparisons are bibliographic scholarship, separate from the formal theorem assertions.
The BFGS update, secant equation, positive-definiteness preservation and determinant identity are
classical. Trace/determinant analysis is developed in Nocedal [14, Section 5, equations (5.7), (5.15)
and (5.21)] and the earlier Byrd–Nocedal work [15]. The determinant endpoint in this chapter
belongs to that established theory. The complete adjacent-degree principal-minor inequality is
distinguished from it; the literature audit found no exact match, without establishing publication
priority. Gram determinants, Schur complements and Cauchy–Binet are standard tools.
Product bounds and arithmetic–geometric mean already occur in BFGS convergence proofs:
see Schöpfer [11, proof of Theorem 3.7] and Jin–Jiang–Mokhtari [18, Appendices C–D]. Their
smooth hypotheses and normalized quantities differ from those in this chapter. Here the actual
principal-minor hierarchy supplies reciprocal bounds for qk = gkT Hk gk , including the finite-horizon
and summable-decrease conclusions; those specific conclusions were not located in the accessed
literature.
The energy cancellations in this chapter are algebraic consequences of the classical Hessian update [14,
Section 5]; congruence and quadratic-form inequalities are established tools. Their use in a moving-
frame criterion still requires the separately stated visibility, secant and transport hypotheses.

