point and matrix places the same finite obstruction arbitrarily close to the minimizer. It is not an
infinite admissible run and does not contradict the positive theorems.

Relation to earlier work
The following comparisons are bibliographic scholarship, separate from the formal theorem assertions.
Guo–Lewis [10, Theorem 4.1 and Corollary 4.4] uses smooth extension and proves a weaker
subsequential conclusion for u2 + |v|. The theorem in this chapter gives full convergence for that
example within its centered family. Its smooth secant estimate uses established cocoercivity [4,
Theorem 2.5]: local regularity is used on an open convex neighborhood, with compact buffers
and, where needed, an enlarged constant. It does not require a globally Lipschitz gradient or
minimization of each original summand at the center.
Invertible affine invariance is established in Lewis–Overton [2, Section 2] and Xie–Wächter [1,
Theorem II.1]. The rectangular reduction in this chapter additionally identifies the point selected
on an affine fiber. Schöpfer’s smooth noncompact convergence results [11, Theorems 3.4 and 3.7]
do not identify that selector. Exact publication priority for this formula remains unresolved; no
originality claim is made.
Related quantitative work includes smooth strongly convex Armijo–Wolfe rates [18], smooth exact-
search rates [19], and smooth self-concordant analysis [20]. These assumptions and line searches
differ from the nonsmooth settings in this chapter. Their matrix-potential techniques are precedents,
but their rate theorems do not imply these estimates. The present rates count accepted iterations,
with no uniform bound on line-search trials.
Lewis–Overton [2, Theorem 4.5 and Proposition 4.11] supplies scalar Armijo–weak-Wolfe existence
theory. Differentiability of the restriction to a ray does not imply full-space differentiability at
acceptance. The existence argument and the finite obstruction in this chapter preserve that
distinction; neither asserts an infinite-run failure.




