The objective-value theorem below holds more generally for every finite convex, positively ho-
mogeneous function on the whole space. “Finite” in this broader statement means real-valued
everywhere, not a finite number of pieces.
Proof status. The value and iterate statements below have reviewed Lean proofs under their
stated assumptions. Their formal hypotheses and dependencies are indexed with the source claims.
Finite numerical tests and publication-priority assertions are not premises of those proofs. The
reusable conditional lifting implications retain any projected-run limit explicitly stated in their
hypotheses.

7.1.1 Theorem 7.A: an unconditional objective-value dichotomy
Let f : Rn → R be finite, convex, and positively homogeneous of degree one. Every infinite
admissible full-BFGS run satisfies

                                  f (xk ) −→ 0     or   f (xk ) −→ −∞.

There is no assumption that the iterates, displacements, accepted step lengths, or matrices are
bounded. There is also no assumption of a finite gradient alphabet for this theorem.
Consequently, if f is bounded below, then f (xk ) → 0 = min f . If f is coercive, then xk → 0,
its unique minimizer. If an infinite run ever attains f (xk ) ≤ 0, its values must tend to −∞; the
finite-value branch has f (xk ) > 0 at every iteration.

7.1.2 Theorem 7.B: a many-piece extension of the common-tilt geometry
For a finite max-linear objective, let G be the set of gradients occurring infinitely often. If the
objective values have a finite limit, then

                                                 0 ∈ aff G.

This no longer needs a bounded-displacement hypothesis. In particular,


