In the first alternative the limit is nonstationary. The previously constructed exact three-piece
example attains that alternative. In the second, convergence of the directions xk /∥xk ∥ is not
asserted.
More generally, for any finite max-linear objective, if the span of the recurrent gradients has
dimension at most two, every finite-value-limit run has convergent iterates. Section 7.7 proves this
broader statement, including the noncoercive bounded-below case.

7.1.4 What this does not yet establish
The objective-value classification is now unconditional in every dimension. The remaining unresolved
iterate question in this investigation is the zero-value branch for noncompact objectives when the
recurrent gradient span has dimension at least three. We have neither proved that all such iterate
sequences converge nor constructed a nonconvergent zero-value-limit trajectory.
This distinction is essential: the results close the previous unbounded-displacement gap for function
values, but are not being presented as a full all-dimensional classification of the iterates.

