7.5.4 Bounded-below and coercive consequences
A positively homogeneous function bounded below cannot take a negative value: otherwise f (tx) =
tf (x) → −∞. Thus its minimum value is zero, attained at the origin, and Theorem 7.A gives
unconditional objective convergence.
If its sublevels are bounded, then f (x) > 0 for x ̸= 0. Compactness of the unit sphere gives
f (x) ≥ µ∥x∥ for some µ > 0, so fk → 0 forces xk → 0.
These consequences now hold for all finite convex sublinear objectives, not only finite polyhedral
maxima. No rate or finite-path-length estimate is asserted.

