7.7.2 A two-gradient endpoint observation
Assume fk → 0. Then dk → 0, and every endpoint satisfies

                        ⊤
                       gk+1 xk+1 = fk+1 −→ 0,        gk⊤ xk+1 = fk − dk −→ 0.                   (7.7.3)

In two dimensions, if gk and gk+1 are linearly independent, these two equations determine xk+1 .
There are only finitely many distinct pairs, so their inverse norms are uniformly bounded. Thus
the endpoints of all independent-gradient transitions converge to zero, even if the full sequence was
not known to be bounded.
An unchanged nonzero gradient cannot satisfy weak Wolfe on a strict descent step. Distinct
gradients that are positive multiples of one another also cannot both be active at positive-value
iterates: among positive multiples of a slope, only the largest multiple can attain a positive
maximum. Consequently, any dependent transition on the positive-value branch is between opposite
slopes.

7.7.3 The unbounded-below planar case
If the global objective is unbounded below, its slope set cannot contain an opposite pair a, −κa
with κ > 0: their maximum alone is nonnegative everywhere. Every accepted gradient transition is
therefore linearly independent in the plane.
Equation (7.3) now gives xk → 0 directly. Combined with Theorem 7.A, this proves Theorem
7.C. In the other branch, global Lipschitz continuity gives |f (x)| ≤ M ∥x∥, so fk → −∞ implies
∥xk ∥ → ∞.
For an unbounded-below max-linear objective there is no stationary point: a stationary point of
a convex function would be a global minimizer, and no minimizer exists. Thus the zero limit is
genuinely nonstationary.

