Proof of Corollary 9.3. Remove branches inactive at x∗ by shrinking the neighborhood. Form the
augmented matrix A(x) whose columns are (∇fi (x), 1) for i ∈ I∗ . Affine independence makes A(x∗ )
full column rank; that rank persists. Whenever A(p)λ = (0, 0, 1)T has a solution, it is unique and
equals
                                  (A(p)T A(p))−1 A(p)T (0, 0, 1)T .
This is continuous and agrees with the positive vector λ∗ at x∗ . Shrink the neighborhood so
every component remains positive. An affine representation of zero using the gradients active at
p, extended by zero coefficients on inactive indices, must equal this vector. It therefore has no
inactive indices and is a convex representation of zero. This proves local (9.4). This geometric
argument is the one recorded in Chapter 8, with related nondegeneracy arguments in [5].
In the plane at most three gradients are affinely independent. At a non-affine-critical nearby point,
three cannot all be active, since their affine hull is R2 . A singleton gives a smooth chart. For two
active gradients, the difference is nonzero; if the gradients were linearly dependent, their affine line
would contain zero, again contradicting non-affine-criticality. The other branches have strict gaps
at that point, so the required local two-branch representation follows.
Choose a low sublevel contained in this neighborhood. Its strictly convex positive sublevels and the
regular-switching property allow Theorem 9.1, then Corollary 9.2. The localization uses only the
objective, not the line-search constants or the initial matrix. Uniformly strongly convex branches
on a convex local neighborhood make their maximum strongly convex there, supplying the sufficient
strict-contour condition.

9.7.1 A tame-geometric alternative
Chapter 8 proves that a finite convex semialgebraic function has finitely many affine-critical values.
Its proof uses a finite piecewise-smooth cover of the lifted critical graph, so the point path and
its actual objective composition have simultaneous derivatives off a finite exceptional set. The
two-sided supporting inequality and an affine combination of supporting vectors equal to zero then
force the composition derivative to vanish. Continuity joins the resulting constants. No finite-value,
path-cover, or composition-differentiability premise is assumed in the final result.
The actual affine-critical set also has finitely many piecewise-smooth path classes, each semialgebraic
and internally piecewise-smoothly connected. Once finite critical values are known, continuity
makes the value image of each such class a singleton, so F is constant along every piecewise-
smooth path in the critical set. This avoids presuming that an arbitrary smooth point path has a
semialgebraic graph or that its composition with a nonsmooth F is differentiable in advance. These
path classes are distinguished from ordinary topological connected components, whose finiteness,
semialgebraicity and value constancy are proved separately.
Combining A’s low affine-critical gap with Theorem 9.1 gives the following restricted planar basin
conclusion. On a compact structural sublevel above an attained minimum, retain strictly convex
positive sublevels and the regular smooth/two-branch hypotheses away from affine criticality. Then
a sufficiently low objective basin gives convergence in value and distance to the full minimizer set;
uniqueness gives convergence of the whole sequence. Alternatively, with a unique minimizer and the
structural hypotheses on a neighborhood, localization supplies a sufficiently low trapped compact
sublevel. In both versions the basin is chosen before the admissible run, its initial SPD matrix and
its fixed valid line-search constants. No positive-multiplier condition is needed. Semialgebraicity
alone does not give the dynamical conclusion: degenerate noncritical ties and flat contours remain
outside this theorem.




