9.9 What can already be proved in any dimension
There is a useful partial statement before transport. Let K be a compact set containing no affine-
critical point. Suppose that a finite convex F has an exact finite maximum representation by convex
C 2 branches on a neighborhood of every point of K. For each p ∈ K, let J = I(p) be the fixed
family active at p, and assume its gradient-difference matrix has constant rank on a neighborhood
of p. Nearby inactive branches can be removed by their strict gaps. Affine independence of the
family at p is a sufficient condition for this local rank hypothesis. The noncritical restriction on K
is essential: unit pairing with all subgradients is impossible at an affine-critical point.
The minimum-norm point aJ (x) of the affine hull of these gradients is C 1 in a smaller chart and
remains nonzero. To see the regularity, choose an affinely independent subfamily at p that spans
this affine hull. Independence persists nearby, and constant rank makes the same subfamily span
the full fixed-family hull nearby. The inverse-Gram formula for its minimum-norm point is therefore

