Relation to earlier work
The following comparisons are bibliographic scholarship, separate from the formal theorem assertions.
Gebken’s published Theorem 3.1 assumes an iterate limit and the matrix behavior (B1), including
spectral bounds and annihilation of gradient differences [5]. The assertions in this chapter derive
their cluster conclusions. Lewis–Wylie’s local Newton theory instead concerns a bundle algorithm [6].
These are related results with different hypotheses or algorithms; the literature audit does not
establish publication priority for the full statements in this chapter.
The normal-angle expression hn + hθ T and its derivative cancellation are classical; see Bogosel,
Section 2, equation (4) [23]. Here h is a glued comparison function, without an asserted convex-
support-function property.
The multiplier argument in this chapter uses the established nondegeneracy geometry discussed in
Chapter 8; compare [6, 5]. Its full-BFGS application additionally requires the preceding dynamical
theorem.



