Fixed normal geometry in arbitrary dimension
A fixed space of jump normals leaves controlled smooth directions in arbitrary dimension. Residual
transport can be paid for in those directions; this is a different hypothesis from planar switching
geometry.

11.1 A concrete dimension-independent local theorem
Let a ∈ Rn be a unit vector, b ∈ R, and write

                         P = {x : aT x = b},         S = a⊥ ,     PS = I − aaT .

The normal direction is fixed; the dimension n − 1 of the tangent space is unrestricted.
Theorem 11.1 (One exceptional hyperplane). Let F : Rn → R be finite and convex, with
unique minimizer x∗ ∈ P . Let K = {F ≤ F (x0 )} be compact. On an open convex neighborhood of
K, assume:

   1. F is µ-strongly convex for some µ > 0.
   2. All subgradients have the same tangential projection,

                                          PS g = A(x)      (g ∈ ∂F (x)),

      where A is Lipschitz continuous on that neighborhood.
   3. F is locally C 1,1 away from P .

Then every infinite admissible full-BFGS run starting at x0 satisfies

                                         F (xk ) → F∗ ,    xk → x∗ .

The hyperplane itself may include differentiable points; it is allowed to contain failures of gradient-
Lipschitz regularity as well as actual kinks. No uniform bound on the full gradient-Lipschitz
constants as the hyperplane is approached is required.
If these hypotheses hold only in a neighborhood of a unique minimizer, some ε > 0 gives the same
conclusion for every start with F (x0 ) < F∗ + ε. The same basin works for every SPD initialization
and every fixed valid line-search pair. Compact sublevel trapping supplies this local statement:
choose a low sublevel contained in the structural neighborhood, then use Armijo monotonicity.
The assumptions concern the objective. In particular, the claim that a part of Bk = Hk−1 has a
controlled growth rate will be derived, not assumed.

11.1.1 A simple family and a weaker-curvature version
