11.2 A forced affine-critical cluster-point theorem
The method also fits the affine-criticality program directly.
Theorem 11.2 (Parallel singular interfaces). Let F be finite convex with attained minimum
and compact sublevel K. Assume F is µ-strongly convex on an open convex neighborhood of K.
Let a be a unit vector and E ⊂ R closed. On that neighborhood suppose:

   • the singular set is Σ = {x : aT x ∈ E}, every point of Σ is genuinely nondifferentiable, and F
     is locally C 1,1 off Σ;
   • PS ∂F (x) = {A(x)} for a Lipschitz field A, where S = a⊥ .

Then the cluster set of every infinite admissible full-BFGS run in K contains a point p with

                                             0 ∈ aff ∂F (p).

There may be several or countably many parallel interfaces, subject to the closed-set and regularity
assumptions above. No finite affine-critical set is assumed.
At a point of Σ, the subdifferential is a nontrivial compact segment parallel to a, because every
subgradient has the same S projection. Hence

