on K, or on a sufficiently low trapped neighborhood, gives convergence to the minimum value and,
under uniqueness, convergence of the whole sequence.
Theorem 11.1 is slightly different from a literal specialization of Theorem 11.2: it allows the
exceptional hyperplane to contain smooth but not locally C 1,1 points. Having x∗ ∈ P makes every
zero of A on P optimal, so genuine nondifferentiability at every exceptional point is unnecessary
