Relation to earlier work
The following comparisons are bibliographic scholarship, separate from the formal theorem assertions.
The unit-first search in this chapter is Lewis–Overton’s Algorithm 4.6 [2]. Earlier negative results
include their convex subgradient/exact-search construction with null steps [28], smooth globally
nonconvex examples [31, 32, 33, 34, 35], Clarke-stationary limits with descent directions [29], and
scaled memoryless BFGS failure [30]. Those settings differ from the convex, differentiable-query,
full-update statement here. The recent planar counterexample of Diao uses a smooth nonconvex
objective and an escaping exact-search trajectory [36]. No exact match for the complete theorem
was identified in the documented search; this is not a priority certification.
Prescribing an algebraic orbit and then constructing an objective that realizes its update equations
has substantial precedents in Mascarenhas’s divergence constructions [31, 32, 33]. The convex
interpolation and all-query containment in this chapter are specific additional properties. Precondi-
tioned residual maps and rigorous root enclosures are standard validated-numerics tools; compare
Rump [37, Theorem 13.3]. Here the required rational inequalities have their own Lean proofs, with
no external numerical computation imported as a proof.




