Relation to earlier work
The following comparisons are bibliographic scholarship, separate from the formal theorem assertions.
The negative-results comparisons in Chapter 12 apply here with the stated stronger convexity and
different branch regularity. This chapter uses the fixed line-search pair displayed in this chapter;
D’s wider parameter family is not imported. The first three branches of the local finite-branch
construction fail to be twice differentiable at the accumulation point. The simpler global quadratic-
plus-maximum example does not establish failure arbitrarily near its true minimizer. These
distinctions are essential when comparing the theorem with smooth nonconvex counterexamples
[32, 33, 36].
The stable-manifold principle and graph-transform method are classical; this chapter already uses
Dyatlov’s exposition [3, Sections 3–4]. The report-specific work identifies the derivative of the
actual BFGS return map, certifies its spectral bounds, and applies the principle to that map. Its
exact formal orbit construction is recorded with the proof correspondence.
The final change of coordinates fixing the common SPD initialization to identity uses the established
affine invariance of full BFGS; see Lewis–Overton [2, Section 2] and Xie–Wächter [1, Theorem II.1].
It is a single fixed transformation for the constructed family, not a per-iteration algorithm change.




