14.3.1 D-root
Actual algebraic seed existence and isolation, denominator signs, accepted and rejected trial
decisions, and the global embedding geometry are proved. The root and containment statements
apply to the exact specified objects, rather than a rounded seed.

14.3.2 D-symbolic
The inverse and Hessian updates, their secant identities, determinant and positivity properties, and
the literal seed-cycle closure are Lean equalities or inequalities. No symbolic-program output is
accepted as a mathematical premise.

14.3.3 D-full-oracle
The exact deterministic failure and embedded local failure theorems establish the actual run and
every stated line-search trial. They imply the specified infinite behavior; a finite list of sampled
trials is not their proof.

14.3.4 L-hyperbolicity
The normalized Jacobian is the derivative of the actual return map. The spectral contract
and rational precision statements are proved for that derivative, and the actual stable-manifold
construction is linked to it. The embedding’s Hessian claims retain their stated local scope.

14.3.5 L-embedding
Scalar node, peak, ramp and regularity formulas prove the complete local model. The smooth and
step constructions then give their respective actual failure copies. The two regularity constructions
retain their distinct hypotheses and conclusions.

14.3.6 A-minors
Compression identities and the Gram numerator are proved in arbitrary finite dimension. The
principal Gram-sum identity and full-BFGS principal-minor hierarchy use actual determinants.
Selected finite-dimensional cases are not substituted for this theorem.

14.3.7 H-lifting
Actual projected full updates and the invariant matrix range give affine-fiber lifting. Reusable
conditional limit implications retain their projected-run hypotheses. The final low-rank applications
derive the limits they use.

14.3.8 M-mixed
The actual full-matrix hierarchy yields the normalized hierarchy, weighted potential, qualitative
reciprocal growth and superquadratic reciprocal growth under summable predicted decreases. A
separately specified unexcited-metric example proves its literal matrix-limit statement; no such
limit is assumed in the principal convergence framework.

14.3.9 P-polyhedral
The smooth-plus-polyhedral and smooth PLQ basin theorems, linear-image lifting and the stated
scalar regularity example are formal results. Their hypotheses distinguish local smoothness from
the stronger twice-differentiable conditions that some examples fail.

14.3.10 R-secant
Finite maxima, mixed powers, inward inequalities and homogeneous gauge composition lead to
the stated radial basins and actual-run convergence. The exponents, domains and differentiability
assumptions are present in the exact theorem types.




