A.3 Sound enclosure of every query
The remaining task is finite because one cycle has only nine queries. For each query we must certify
its unique active branch, the descent direction at the current iterate, and the comparison that
accepts or rejects it. A positive derivative at each accepted query supplies weak Wolfe for every
permitted curvature constant. Strict value increase at each rejected query makes Armijo fail, so
the routine reaches exactly the next displayed bisection. This explains why the certificate below
contains more than values at the three accepted points.
An interval [a, b] encloses the corresponding real quantity. Addition, negation, multiplication and
reciprocal are evaluated by

                         [a, b] + [c, d] = [a + c, b + d],     −[a, b] = [−b, −a],
                             [a, b][c, d] = [min(ac, ad, bc, bd), max(ac, ad, bc, bd)],

and [a, b]−1 = [1/b, 1/a] when 0 ∈ / [a, b]. These inclusion rules follow from order on the reals;
induction on a rational expression proves the soundness of every resulting bound. All endpoints
used here are rational, so no directed floating-point rounding is needed.
For the query calculation it suffices to use the larger box

                    [318481, 318482]            [−1025233, −1025232]                 [6019, 6021]
               c∈                    ,     d∈                        ,        ρ∈                  .       (A.4)
                          106                           106                              105
The first two inclusions follow from the root box; substitution in (12.3), with positive denominator,
gives the third. Evaluate zj from (12.6) and use

                    p0 = (−1, 0)T ,    p1 = (127/256, 1/16)T ,      p2 = 16ρ(1 − c, −d)T .

For a row with active index i, set q = zj + αpj and evaluate the following rational expressions by
the inclusion rules:

                                                      gjT zj + αgjT pj /3 − giT q,    acceptance,
                                                    (
              (gi − gk ) q(k ̸= i),
                         T
                                       −αgjT pj ,
                                                      giT q − gjT zj ,                rejection.

For an acceptance also evaluate αgiT pj . Every expression in its row is strictly greater than the
following positive rational lower bound, in the order of Table 12.3:
                              1
                                 (729, 2417, 145, 75, 5964, 2682, 1041, 221, 20).                         (A.5)
                             104
The same interval operations give the value enclosures in that table. For the three base points,
the designated active gaps are positive as well. Evaluating 3 ± giT q for every i, 2 − q2 , and the
designated active value giT q gives positive lower bounds for all base points and all nine queries.
This proves (12.7). All inputs, expressions, interval rules and output bounds of this finite certificate
are now specified. The strict active gaps also give differentiability on a neighborhood of each query.
Together with the matrix identities, these calculations complete the proof of the seed and query
assertions in Chapter 12.




